<h2 id="wg-010">Use familiar, concrete words</h2>

Use the shortest word that keeps the technical meaning. Keep a technical term when it is the exact label.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Initiate the pairing procedure. | Start pairing. |
| The actuator is de-energised. | The socket reports that it is off. |

<h2 id="wg-013">Make sentences easy to follow</h2>

One idea per sentence. Put a condition before the action it limits.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The schedule is unable to be saved as a result of the connection having been interrupted. | Home Assistant could not save the schedule. The connection dropped. |
| The heating stays on if the window sensor reports Closed and the target is above 16°C. | If the window sensor reports Closed and the target is above 16°C, the heating stays on. |

<h2 id="wg-014">Write important conditions in full</h2>

Use “cannot” and “do not” in conditions, warnings and short instructions.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Don’t unplug the hub. | Do not unplug the hub during the update. |

<h2 id="wg-020">Use one word for one operation</h2>

Use the same word for the same operation, so “Delete” never means “Turn off”.

<%= example('wg-household-terms') %>

<h2 id="wg-021">Keep native labels and translations</h2>

Use the label Home Assistant shows, in the language it shows.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Find the identification of the light. | Find the **Entity ID** of the light. |

<h2 id="wg-022">Keep names exactly as people typed them</h2>

Do not change the case, spelling or language of a name someone gave.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Mam’s heater changed to Mother’s heater | Mam’s heater |

<h2 id="wg-025">Keep literal values literal</h2>

Put entity IDs, keys and paths in code formatting. Do not change characters inside them.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Entity ID: light.reading-lamp | Entity ID: `light.reading_lamp` |

